{"posts":[{"id":"finddecision_p13_301","t":"Edge wrote: Hi Nytewyre,. I noticed you sometimes marked the decision to break which is the base just before a barrier like S/R. I am giving you this information because I wasted so much time on conventional S/D trading which teaches these areas are true supply and demand. Erase that from your mind. You will even see how RTM has changed over time. What makes this place great is the fact that even IF and other Masters never sit idle with how they see the market. They adapt. You will love it here!!!\n\nThe true DPs occur seconds after the barrier is broken and it retraces. Its often hidden in the tick chart and makes for a great trap for those S/D traders who swear by peaks and valleys as true supply/demand. The less visible our DP kink the better, especially if it retraces and forms a high/low which makes for a great FO zone.\n\nDo you mean like the image in the FTR article in markepedia?","a":"—","d":"","n":"#301","p":13,"im":[],"m":301},{"id":"finddecision_p13_302","t":"Hi edge. Thank you for the challenging my thought process. I am new to trading so I want to apologize beforehand if I can't understand some of the terms that you are using. I have been thinking about decision points for the past week. I marked the decision to break bases before price pushes through the barrier because I thought that is the most cost effective place to break a barrier, any further away from that there would be other areas of supply and demand which would prevent price from advancing beyond the barrier. I thought the institutional traders would wait until price comes close to the barrier before putting in their big orders to push price beyond the barrier. Anyways, I will study harder to understand where true DPs occur.\n\nRegards CQ","a":"—","d":"","n":"#302","p":13,"im":[],"m":302},{"id":"finddecision_p13_303","t":"Edge\n\nTheBeard wrote:\n\nEdge wrote: Hi Nytewyre,. I noticed you sometimes marked the decision to break which is the base just before a barrier like S/R. I am giving you this information because I wasted so much time on conventional S/D trading which teaches these areas are true supply and demand. Erase that from your mind. You will even see how RTM has changed over time. What makes this place great is the fact that even IF and other Masters never sit idle with how they see the market. They adapt. You will love it here!!!\n\n1373\n\nThe true DPs occur seconds after the barrier is broken and it retraces. Its often hidden in the tick chart and makes for a great trap for those S/D traders who swear by peaks and valleys as true supply/demand. The less visible our DP kink the better, especially if it retraces and forms a high/low which makes for a great FO zone.\n\nYeah Karlos, a couple of the diagrams of the ftr dont retrace past the barrier again. I think the concept has changed a bit, as historically it was the cap or hook considered the FTR but they really are only the FTB to the invisible FTR kink inside the area that initialy broke the barrier","a":"—","d":"","n":"#303","p":13,"im":[],"m":303},{"id":"finddecision_p13_304","t":"So we can ignore these two FTR examples and look to the kinks instead?","a":"—","d":"","n":"#304","p":13,"im":["943cf25cb3e69f54ec984572902d6f155d66e042"],"m":304},{"id":"finddecision_p13_305","t":"I am starting my DP Homework.","a":"—","d":"","n":"#305","p":13,"im":["6350742f2dd2f8ba138cf9cc754f98c58633b66d","27970cd309f7715bc47a39029ae5731d76cbecfc","771fc48953c8802e54ec7292533e7dd523195ec1","c23d0e0d5f5b30d7c06ed834d0011b1d5288f605","554c4b1a87b197769be09fb4d517be4a1ca57088"],"m":305},{"id":"finddecision_p13_306","t":"","a":"—","d":"","n":"#306","p":13,"im":["a7b4241427e7482389c848a33fc65893204cbcde","9542394983e286d101176daf5cd212ff2716dda2","b884bf471bcb9dd1c823b9ea64549e566e144137","d4d7e18f45ce1d742a196932f0ca306dcb981e6a","3be89e339a0c6a6eb3536f6f88d4d92b4c7f8206","3a54362f0c85a00100e8eb05be60e5f017399186"],"m":306},{"id":"finddecision_p13_307","t":"7.\n\n8